{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC5 (`make verify` green) — first run FAILED, root-caused and fixed\n\n`make verify` at `9906f0ec7b3` returned **rc=2** after 1h55m: **1 failed, 20140 passed, 27 skipped.**\n\n```\nFAILED tests/unit/test_transition_force_flag_24f7.py::test_retired_spelling_is_absent_from_every_tracked_file\nAssertionError: tracked file(s) still use the retired --force-close flag:\n  ['evals/similar-tickets/digests.json']\n```\n\nThis is the class `AGENTS.md` warns about: a whole-tree policy scan enforced by **pytest**, invisible to `make lint` and `make typecheck`, which were both green.\n\n### What the scan actually found\n\nAll 4 occurrences are inside **LLM-generated digest prose summarizing historical tickets** that discuss the flag — not callers:\n\n- `…leaving the ticket uncertified and requiring --force-close.`\n- `…blocking the close of five idea-status stories and an epic entirely without --force-close workaround.`\n- `…force-close approved by operator under session ce54970555`\n- `…Affected tickets cannot earn signed attestations without --force-close workaround.`\n\nThe test's own docstring scopes it to \"stale **callers** in tracked scripts that construct rebar argv\". By that intent these are not offenders; by the byte scan they are.\n\n### Why scrubbing the string is the wrong fix\n\n`digests.json` is the 630-entry cached digest corpus this change commits so the candidates reproduce without model access. **AC1 of this ticket is `measure.py --check` re-deriving the fixture exactly against those bytes.** Rewriting them to dodge a string scan would break the benchmark's reproducibility — the very property AC1 asserts — in order to silence a non-caller.\n\n### The fix\n\nAdded the path to the scan's existing `EXEMPT` set, which already carries precedent for exactly this case:\n\n> `# Frozen LLM-experiment corpora. Their content is recorded experiment INPUT; rewriting them would corrupt the record the experiments' conclusions rest on.`\n\nalongside `e6_selfconsistency_inputs.jsonl`, `exp2_agentic.jsonl`, `operator_attested_ac_corpus.jsonl`, and the mined `plan-review-T6.eval.yaml`.\n\nThe sibling test `test_every_exemption_is_still_earned` requires each exempt path to exist **and still contain the needle**, so this entry cannot rot into a silent blind spot — it fails the day the string leaves the corpus.\n\n### Verification of the fix\n\n| check | result |\n|---|---|\n| `pytest tests/unit/test_transition_force_flag_24f7.py` | **14 passed** |\n| `check_mechanism_delta.py --check` | rc=0 |\n| `check_complexity_baseline.py --check` | rc=0 |\n| `check_comment_hygiene.py` | rc=0 |\n| `ruff check` / `ruff format --check` | clean |\n\nAmended to **`938e962c5d8`**, Change-Id `Ic7685ee911fcd59d42ac60a868f2cda57446ff71` preserved (not yet pushed, so this is not a new patch set). The commit message now records why the exemption exists.\n\nA full `make verify` re-run is queued behind the next free slot — the host holds at 3 concurrent runs, and 19c3, 8257 and 7a7a currently occupy them. AC5 stays unticked until that returns rc=0; the single-test pass above is not a substitute for the contract."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793512132731805106,"uuid":"3423b25d-ec66-4d83-8842-61ad0a736c22"}